The ''Paleoteius'' fossil material was discovered at the {{gloss|''[[Bonapartenykus]]'' site}} on Arriagada Farm, representing [[River|fluvial]] [[sandstone]] outcrops of the [[Allen Formation]],<ref name="agnolinetal2012">{{Cite journal |last1=Agnolin |first1=Federico L. |last2=Powell |first2=Jaime E. |last3=Novas |first3=Fernando E. |last4=Kundrát |first4=Martin |date=1 June 2012 |title=New alvarezsaurid (Dinosauria, Theropoda) from uppermost Cretaceous of north-western Patagonia with associated eggs |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0195667111001923 |journal=[[Cretaceous Research]] |language=en |volume=35 |pages=33–56 |bibcode=2012CrRes..35...33A |doi=10.1016/j.cretres.2011.11.014 |issn=0195-6671 |url-access=subscription|hdl=11336/272003 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> {{convert|70|km|mi}} south of the city of [[General Roca, Río Negro|General Roca]] in [[Río Negro Province]], Argentina. The specimen is housed in the [[:es:Museo Patagónico de Ciencias Naturales Juan Carlos Salgado|Museo Patagónico de Ciencias Naturales]] in General Roca, where it is permanently [[Accession number (cultural property)|accessioned]] as specimen MPCN-PV-1183. The specimen consists of a partial skull and skeleton belonging to a single individual. The cranial material includes the left [[Maxilla#In other animals|maxillae]], [[Frontal bone#Other animals|frontal]], [[Parietal bone#In other animals|parietal]], part of the [[Jugal bone#In reptiles|jugal]], parts of the [[Quadrate bone|quadrate]], the right [[Prefrontal bone|prefrontal]], and various unidentified fragments. The identified postcranial remains include three complete, articulated [[trunk vertebrae]] and an isolated presacral vertebral centrum, the bottom part of the left [[humerus]], the top parts of both [[femora]], and other fragments.<ref name="Paleoteius" />

In 2026, Federico L. Agnolín and colleagues [[Species description|described]] ''Paleoteius lakui'' as a new genus and species of lizard based on these fossil remains, establishing MPCN-PV-1183 as the [[holotype]] specimen. The [[Genus|generic name]], ''Paleoteius'', combines the [[Ancient Greek]] word {{lang|grc|παλαιός}} ({{lang|grc|palaiós}}), meaning {{gloss|old}}, with {{gloss|teius}}, derived from the [[Tupi–Guarani languages|Tupi–Guarani]] word ''teyú'', the most common native term for squamates in that region. The [[Specific name (zoology)|specific name]], ''lakui'', is derived from the [[Mapudungun]] word {{lang|arn|laku}}, meaning {{gloss|grandfather}}.<ref name="Paleoteius" />

